/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Receivers are judged on four properties.
   - Most broadcast receivers use high-side injection: the LO runs above the signal.
   - fLO = fs + fIF, fsi = fs + 2fIF
   - for high-side injection: fs is the wanted signal, fLO the local oscillator and fsi the image, all in hertz.
   - The image fsi lies fIF above the LO, so it too gives fsi − fLO = fIF at the mixer output.
   - A single tuned circuit of loaded quality factor Q, tuned to fs, passes the image weaker than the signal by the image-rejection ratio α:
   - α = √(1 + Q²ρ²), ρ = fsi/fs − fs/fsi
   - α is a voltage ratio (20 log α in dB); ρ is the fractional detuning of the image.
   - For a fixed IF, ρ shrinks as fs rises, because the image is a smaller fraction away.
   - One knob tunes the RF circuit and the LO together on a ganged capacitor, keeping fLO − fs = fIF across the band.
*/
