/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each channel is given a slot of the spectrum.
   - BT = N·Bch + (N − 1)·Bg
   - guard bands only between channels: N channels of modulated bandwidth Bch each, with N − 1 guard bands of Bg hertz.
   - BT = N(Bch + Bg)
   - the slot convention of telephone systems: every channel owns a slot of Bch + Bg, with its guard band inside.
   - With SSB, Bch = W; with DSB-SC or AM, Bch = 2W.
   - The receiver reverses this: a bank of band-pass filters separates the slots, each slot is coherently detected with its own carrier fci, and a low-pass filter returns the voice.
   - Crosstalk, one channel heard in another, has three causes: filters with too little stop-band rejection, carriers that drift towards a neighbour, and nonlinearity in the summer or line amplifiers, whose intermodulation products fall into oth
   - Analog telephony built FDM in stages.
   - The same idea is everywhere in radio.
*/
